// f026_addr_diff_print.fl -- ENTRY-POINT PIN for the v1.4.27 fix of "an ADDRESS-CHAIN DIFFERENCE used
//   DIRECTLY as a print segment used to be REJECTED by real flint".
//
// THE DEFECT THIS ROW NAILS (it was a real flint defect, NOT a documented boundary; boot was right):
//   int main() { int ia[8]; ...; print(["d=", (ia + 8) - ia, "\n"]); return 0; }
//   before v1.4.27 -- real flint : COMPILE ERROR, exit 1, from src\codegen.c:1819-1827, whose message
//       translates as: "an address value cannot go into an int parameter -- parameter 1 of function
//       '_flint_out_int' is a value type (int/char/double), but the argument is a pointer/address
//       (dereference *p first, or change the parameter to a pointer type)"
//     boot : compiles and prints `d=8`, exit 0
//   after v1.4.27 -- BOTH ends compile and print `d=8`, exit 0, byte-identical stdout.
//
// ROOT CAUSE (read before changing; the fix is deliberately LOCAL):
//   src\codegen.c:708-709 `is_addr_expr` decides ND_ADD/ND_SUB by `lhs || rhs`, i.e. "either side is an
//   address => the whole node is an address".  That rule is right for a chain like `*(a + i*8)` but
//   WRONG for `addr - addr`, where the result is a SCALAR difference.  src\codegen.c:773-775 already
//   carries that exception in `is_addr_value` (its ND_SUB arm, codegen.c:773-775: "address minus
//   address = a difference, a scalar, not an address"), but the call-site
//   width check at :1809 -- `is_addr_value(chk) || is_addr_expr(chk)` -- had no such exception, so the
//   scalar difference was misread as an address and handed to the "address into a value parameter" error.
//   FIX = the same exception, applied at that ONE call site (helper `addr_diff_is_scalar`, codegen.c,
//   just above `emit_arg_rax_to_gp`), NOT a change to `is_addr_expr` itself: `is_addr_expr` also drives
//   the 64-bit "wide" math selection (:1233-1236) and the "move whole 64 bits" / movsxd split
//   (:1251/:1266/:1275), and
//   changing it globally would break today-correct scalar paths (`int d = (ia + 8) - ia;` is 8).
//
// WHY THE PROBE HAS FOUR SEGMENTS (bracketing = the self-check that localises the fix):
//   d  = (ia + 8) - ia   the repro form -- MEMORY-BACKED address chain, the exact shape that used to be rejected
//   e  = (p + 8) - p     the POINTER-VARIABLE form of the same chain (the brief's second shape)
//   f  = (ia + 8) - ia   stored into an int first -- the scalar path that was ALREADY correct (must not regress)
//   g  = (p + 1) - p     a second scalar difference, so `8` cannot be a coincidence of one expression
//   s  = ia[0]           a plain int read, proving the array really holds the data (not an accidental 0)
//   Under FLINT's documented bare-pointer byte stepping (decision 40, SPEC.md:262/486) `ia + 8` is
//   `(char*)ia + 8` BYTES, so `(ia + 8) - ia` is 8 -- not 1.  f025_ptr_arith already pins that rule;
//   this row pins that the SAME expression is ACCEPTED as a print segment on both ends.
//
// CORRECT OUTPUT (computed from the rule, not from a run; `d=8` is the pinned part):
//   d=8 e=8 f=8 g=1 s=1
//   `contains:` pins `d=8 e=8 f=8 g=1 s=1`; the runner's -Compare additionally requires the two ends'
//   raw stdout bytes to be IDENTICAL.  The probe prints no pointer value (ASLR-safe: no addresses leak).
//
// MEASURED 2026-10-15 (v1.4.27, raw bytes out of the runner; both ends identical, program exit 0):
//   flint : 20 bytes  d=8 e=8 f=8 g=1 s=1 + LF  (stdout_bytes=20, first_diff_byte=-1 vs boot)
//           hex  64 3d 38 20 65 3d 38 20 66 3d 38 20 67 3d 31 20 73 3d 31 0a
//   boot  : 20 bytes  same hex, byte for byte
//   BEFORE the fix (same probe path, old compiler from %TEMP%\flint-slice-compilerfix\flint-before.exe,
//   the two-line form `print(["d=", (ia + 8) - ia, "\n"])`): real flint refused to compile (exit 1,
//   message quoted above), boot printed `d=8` and exited 0.
//
// HOW THE EXPECTED VALUES WERE COMPUTED (now, never hand-typed):
//   $ia0 = 1; $ia8 = 1   -> d = 8 (byte stepping), g = 1
//   [Text.Encoding]::ASCII.GetBytes("d=8 e=8 f=8 g=1 s=1`n").Length  -> 20
//
// REGRESSION GUARDS (what must turn this row red, and it should not):
//   1. the call-site exception is removed -> real flint refuses again (compile exit 1);
//   2. `is_addr_expr` is "fixed" globally instead -> the 64-bit wide path changes and/or the already
//      correct scalar path breaks (f is the segment watching that: it must stay 8);
//   3. decision-40 byte stepping is turned into C element scaling -> d/e/f become 1 and f025 goes red too.
//
// REVIEW COMMAND (one command, two ends -- both legs must exit 0 and their stdout bytes must be equal):
//   powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "E:\FLINT-lab\test\facts\facts-run.ps1" -Probe f026_addr_diff_print -Compare flint,boot
//   criterion: contains  ->  d=8 e=8 f=8 g=1 s=1
//   (the same command also enforces exit=0 on both ends and byte-identical stdout, which is the
//    stronger half of the pin; `contains:` names the text a human reads in the ledger)
// DEPENDENCIES:
//   E:\FLINT-lab\test\facts\f026_addr_diff_print.fl;E:\FLINT-lab\test\facts\facts-run.ps1;
//   E:\FLINT-lab\flint.exe;E:\FLINT-lab\boot\b1_emit.exe;
//   E:\FLINT-lab\src\codegen.c;E:\FLINT-lab\SPEC.md
//   (context pin for the same expression, byte-stepping rule: E:\FLINT-lab\test\facts\f025_ptr_arith.fl)
//
// ASCII only, no BOM; prints no pointer value -- only scalar differences and one array element.
int main() {
  int ia[8];
  ia[0] = 1; ia[1] = 2; ia[2] = 3; ia[3] = 4;
  int *p = ia;
  int f = (ia + 8) - ia;          // the already-correct scalar path (must not regress)
  print(["d=", (ia + 8) - ia,     // the repro: memory-backed address chain, printed directly
         " e=", (p + 8) - p,      // the repro: pointer-variable form
         " f=", f,
         " g=", (p + 1) - p,
         " s=", ia[0], "\n"]);
  return 0;
}
